/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Planet Earth (batch z) */
WB.L("year-without-summer", "geography", "intermediate", "1816: the year without a summer",
  "A mountain in Indonesia blew itself apart, and a year later it snowed in New England in June. Here is how one eruption chilled half the world.",
  [
    ["A mountain loses its head", "In April 1815 Mount Tambora in Indonesia produced the largest eruption in recorded history.",
      "Mount Tambora sits on Sumbawa, in what is now Indonesia. After days of rumbling, it exploded with enormous force around 10 and 11 April 1815. The blasts were heard more than 2,000 kilometres away, and some colonial officials reportedly mistook them for cannon fire and sent troops to investigate.\n\nAsh and rock rose into the stratosphere, and glowing avalanches of hot gas and debris swept down the slopes. The mountain lost well over a kilometre of height, leaving a caldera about six kilometres wide. Volcanologists rate it a 7 on the Volcanic Explosivity Index, a level reached only a handful of times in the past few thousand years."],
    ["The cost on Sumbawa", "The eruption and the famine that followed killed tens of thousands of people.",
      "The eruption itself killed many thousands of people on Sumbawa, buried under ash or caught by scorching flows. The larger toll came afterwards. Ash smothered crops and fouled water across Sumbawa and neighbouring Lombok, and livestock died in great numbers. Starvation and disease followed for months.\n\nExact numbers are impossible to know because regional records were patchy. Estimates of the total death toll commonly run to tens of thousands, and some researchers argue it was higher. The small kingdom of Tambora, its people and language, was largely wiped out. Archaeologists later found houses, pottery and human remains under the ash, earning the site the nickname 'Pompeii of the East'."],
    ["A veil in the sky", "Sulphur gas, not ash, cooled the planet, by forming a haze high in the stratosphere that reflected sunlight.",
      "Ash falls out of the air within days or weeks, so it was not ash that changed the world's weather. The real culprit was sulphur dioxide. Tambora blasted huge amounts of it into the stratosphere, more than ten kilometres up, where there is no rain to wash it out.\n\nThere it reacted with water to form tiny droplets of sulphuric acid, called sulphate aerosols. Winds spread this haze around the globe over the following months. The droplets reflected some incoming sunlight back into space, so less warmth reached the ground. Global average temperatures dropped by somewhere around half a degree. That sounds small, but it was enough to upset growing seasons across the Northern Hemisphere for a year or more."],
    ["Snow in June", "In 1816 summer frosts and cold rain ruined harvests in North America and Europe, and no one knew why.",
      "In New England and eastern Canada, snow fell in early June, and killing frosts returned in July and August, destroying corn and other crops. Farmers who replanted several times watched each attempt fail.\n\nIn western Europe the summer was cold and relentlessly wet. Harvests failed across Britain, Ireland, France and the German lands, grain prices soared and food riots broke out in several countries. Switzerland suffered such hunger that it was one of its worst years in modern times. Typhus spread through a weakened Irish population. Many New England families gave up their rocky farms and moved west. People at the time had no idea why the weather had turned so savage.\n\nThe cold also had an unexpected literary legacy."],
    ["Solving the mystery", "Ice cores later linked Tambora to the cold of 1816, though the full story also involved an earlier eruption and a quiet Sun.",
      "In June 1816 a group of English writers, including Lord Byron, Percy Shelley and Mary Godwin (soon to be Mary Shelley), holidayed beside Lake Geneva. Endless rain kept them indoors, so Byron proposed a ghost story contest. Mary's idea became Frankenstein; Byron's doctor, John Polidori, wrote The Vampyre.\n\nFor a long time the cold itself was a puzzle. In the early twentieth century William Humphreys argued that volcanic dust and gases could cool the climate. Ice cores from Greenland and Antarctica later clinched it, showing a sharp sulphate spike in layers from about 1815 and 1816. But another large eruption, still unidentified, occurred around 1808 or 1809, and the Sun was quiet, so Tambora struck an already cool world."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do volcanologists rate Tambora's 1815 eruption on the Volcanic Explosivity Index?", ["A 5, similar to Mount St Helens","A 6, a few times a century","A 7, among the largest of recent millennia","An 8, the top of the scale"], 2, "Tambora rates a 7, among the largest eruptions of the past few thousand years."],
    [1, "Why is the true death toll from Tambora hard to know?", ["Records were patchy, and famine caused most deaths","The eruption killed no one directly","Everyone on the island survived and later moved","The deaths were never connected to the eruption"], 0, "The larger toll came from starvation and disease, and records were patchy, so estimates run to tens of thousands or more."],
    [2, "What actually cooled the climate after Tambora erupted?", ["Ash blocking sunlight for several years","Steam from the eruption forming thick clouds","Sulphate droplets in the stratosphere reflecting sunlight","Lava cooling the surrounding ocean"], 2, "Sulphur dioxide reached the stratosphere and formed aerosols that reflected sunlight, whereas ash falls out within days or weeks."],
    [3, "What happened in New England in 1816?", ["Record drought and heat ruined the crops","Snow fell in June and frosts returned in July and August","The summer was normal but winter was harsh","Floods washed away the farms"], 1, "Cold and frost destroyed crops in North America, while western Europe had a cold, wet summer and failed harvests."],
    [4, "What does the evidence in ice cores show about the cold years?", ["Layers of volcanic glass from Indonesia","Unusually thick annual snow layers","Trapped bubbles of volcanic steam","A sulphate spike in layers from 1815 and 1816"], 3, "Sulphate from the eruption fell as acid snow at both poles, leaving a chemical fingerprint in the ice."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A volcano in Indonesia once brought June snow to New England. How?"});

WB.L("cloud-types", "geography", "beginner", "Reading the clouds",
  "Every cloud has a name, and most have a message about the weather to come. A young pharmacist worked out how to read them over two hundred years ago.",
  [
    ["The man who named clouds", "Luke Howard's simple Latin cloud names, proposed in 1802, are still the basis of cloud naming today.",
      "Before the early 1800s, clouds were thought too fleeting to classify. Then English pharmacist and amateur weather watcher Luke Howard gave a talk in London in 1802, published the next year, called On the Modifications of Clouds. Borrowing from naturalists who sorted plants and animals into families, he proposed a few Latin names. Cirrus is a curl or wisp of hair. Cumulus is a heap. Stratus is a layer or sheet. Nimbus refers to rain.\n\nThey could be combined, so a heaped rain cloud became cumulonimbus. The scheme was so clear that it spread across Europe, and the writer Goethe admired Howard enough to write poems in his honour."],
    ["How a cloud is born", "Clouds form when rising air cools until its water vapour condenses onto tiny particles, then stays aloft because the droplets are so small.",
      "Air always holds some invisible water vapour, and warm air holds more. When air rises, the pressure drops, so it expands and cools. At its dew point it can no longer hold all its vapour, and the excess condenses into droplets or ice crystals around microscopic specks of sea salt, dust, smoke and pollen. Air can be forced up by the Sun heating the ground, wind pushing it over mountains, or cold air sliding under warm.\n\nA small fair weather cumulus about a kilometre across holds roughly 500 tonnes of water, about a hundred elephants. It floats because that weight is spread over billions of tiny droplets, which fall so slowly that gentle updrafts hold them up."],
    ["High, middle and low", "Clouds are grouped by height and by whether they spread in sheets or pile up in heaps.",
      "The World Meteorological Organization's International Cloud Atlas recognises ten main cloud types, grouped by height. High clouds, roughly five kilometres up or more, are made of ice crystals: wispy cirrus, patchy cirrocumulus and milky cirrostratus.\n\nMiddle clouds take the prefix alto. Altocumulus forms rows of puffy patches, and altostratus is a grey veil through which the Sun looks like a smudged coin. Low clouds include stratus, a flat grey blanket that can sit on hilltops as fog, stratocumulus in lumpy layers, and nimbostratus, the thick, dark sheet behind a long day of steady rain. Cumulus and cumulonimbus are special because they can stretch from low levels to great heights."],
    ["The king of clouds", "Cumulonimbus is the thunderstorm cloud, towering more than ten kilometres high and flattening into an anvil at the top.",
      "On a hot, humid afternoon, a small cumulus can keep growing as warm, moist air surges upwards inside it. Condensing vapour releases heat, which makes the air rise even faster. A towering cloud like this is cumulus congestus. If it keeps going, it becomes a cumulonimbus, whose top can reach twelve kilometres or more, and higher in the tropics.\n\nWhen the rising air hits the stable layer at the base of the stratosphere, it cannot rise further and spreads sideways into a flat anvil. Inside, strong updrafts and downdrafts toss raindrops and ice about, building hail and separating electrical charges. Cumulonimbus brings downpours, damaging winds, lightning and, occasionally, tornadoes."],
    ["Clouds as forecasts", "Watching how clouds change over a few hours can tell you what weather is coming.",
      "Clouds are not perfect forecasters, but their patterns carry real information. High cirrus streaks that thicken into a pale sheet of cirrostratus, sometimes with a halo around the Sun or Moon, often mean a warm front and its rain are approaching within a day or so. Small, flat-topped cumulus on a sunny morning suggest a settled day, but cumulus that swell into tall, cauliflower towers by midday warn of afternoon storms. Lens-shaped lenticular clouds near mountains signal strong winds aloft.\n\nClouds still hold surprises. In 2017 the Atlas added asperitas, a dramatic, wave-like underside first championed by members of the public in the Cloud Appreciation Society."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the cloud name 'cumulus' mean?", ["Layer or sheet","Rain","Curl of hair","Heap"], 3, "Cumulus is Latin for heap, for puffy, piled-up clouds. Stratus means layer and cirrus a curl of hair."],
    [1, "Why does a cloud holding hundreds of tonnes of water not fall?", ["Clouds are made of steam, which is lighter than air","The Earth's magnetic field supports them","It is spread over billions of tiny, slowly falling droplets","Clouds are hollow, like balloons"], 2, "Each droplet is minute, so it sinks extremely slowly and gentle updrafts keep it aloft."],
    [2, "How are the ten main cloud types mainly grouped?", ["By their colour and brightness","By height, and by sheet or heap shape","By the season they appear in","By how much rain they hold"], 1, "High, middle and low clouds are further described as layered (stratus) or heaped (cumulus)."],
    [3, "What causes the flat 'anvil' top of a thunderstorm cloud?", ["Strong upper winds slicing off the top of the cloud","Ice crystals melting and collapsing at the top","Rising air meets the stable stratosphere and spreads out","The cloud cooling quickly after sunset"], 2, "Updrafts cannot push far into the stable stratosphere, so the cloud spreads out into an anvil."],
    [4, "What does thin cirrus thickening into cirrostratus, perhaps with a halo, often signal?", ["A week of settled, fine weather","A warm front and rain approaching","A sudden cold snap with frost","Snow within the next hour"], 1, "Thickening high cloud is a classic sign that a warm front and its rain are on the way, often within a day or so."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A fluffy cloud can weigh as much as a hundred elephants. Why doesn't it fall?"});

WB.L("tornadoes", "geography", "beginner", "Tornadoes: the most violent winds on Earth",
  "Tornadoes produce the fastest winds measured near the ground, yet they are small, brief and still not fully understood. Here is what we do know.",
  [
    ["Born in supercells", "A tornado is a violently rotating column of air from a storm cloud to the ground, and the strongest come from supercells.",
      "A tornado is a column of rapidly spinning air touching both the ground and the base of a thunderstorm. Many show as a funnel because falling pressure condenses moisture and dust thickens the shape. Most last a few minutes and travel a few kilometres, but a few become monsters, hundreds of metres wide and tens of kilometres long.\n\nThe powerful ones form in supercells. Warm, moist air near the ground, cooler, drier air above, a lifting trigger and wind shear (winds changing speed and direction with height) set the lower atmosphere rolling horizontally. A strong updraft tilts that roll upright, so the updraft rotates. This broad column is a mesocyclone. Why only some supercells produce tornadoes is still being researched."],
    ["Tornado Alley", "The United States has more tornadoes than anywhere else because contrasting air masses collide over its flat central plains.",
      "The United States records on the order of a thousand or more tornadoes in a typical year, far more than any other country. Warm, humid air flows north from the Gulf of Mexico, dry air descends from the Rockies and south west deserts, and cold air sweeps down from Canada. They meet over flat plains, with no east to west mountain range to keep them apart.\n\nThe region from Texas to South Dakota is nicknamed Tornado Alley, and the south east sees many deadly tornadoes too. The Tri-State Tornado of 1925 killed nearly 700 people across Missouri, Illinois and Indiana. Bangladesh, however, suffered the deadliest tornado on record, in 1989."],
    ["Measuring the unmeasurable", "Tornadoes are rated by the damage they cause, because their winds are rarely measured directly.",
      "Ordinary instruments are usually destroyed by a tornado, or simply not in the right place. In 1971 the meteorologist Ted Fujita created a scale that estimated wind speed from the damage left behind. The United States replaced it in 2007 with the Enhanced Fujita scale, which runs from EF0, peeling shingles and breaking branches, to EF5, where well built houses are swept off their foundations. EF5 winds are estimated at over 320 kilometres per hour.\n\nPortable Doppler radar has since let scientists chase storms and measure winds inside them. During the Oklahoma outbreak of May 1999, a truck-mounted radar recorded winds of around 480 kilometres per hour, among the highest ever measured on Earth."],
    ["Tornadoes in Australia", "Australia gets tornadoes too, usually weaker than in America, and the outback willy-willy is a different thing.",
      "Many Australians are surprised to learn tornadoes happen here. They are reported every year, most often in the south west of Western Australia, along the eastern seaboard and in parts of inland New South Wales and Queensland, and some are spawned by tropical cyclones coming ashore. Most are short-lived and weak, but destructive ones occur, such as the tornado that tore through Bucca in Queensland in 1992.\n\nA common confusion is worth clearing up. The 'willy-willy' of outback folklore is usually a dust devil, a spinning column of air that forms on hot, clear days from heated ground, not from a storm. Dust devils are mostly harmless curiosities, while true tornadoes need a thunderstorm overhead."],
    ["Staying safe", "Shelter low, inside and away from windows; opening windows to equalise pressure is a myth.",
      "Take warnings seriously. In Australia, follow the Bureau of Meteorology's severe thunderstorm warnings, and call 000 in a life-threatening emergency. If a tornado approaches, go to the lowest floor of a sturdy building and into a small interior room, such as a bathroom or hallway, with as many walls as possible between you and the outside. Stay away from windows, and cover your head and neck. Cars and caravans offer poor protection.\n\nOne old myth deserves retiring. People were once told to open windows so the house would not explode from low pressure. In fact, houses are destroyed by wind and flying debris, and time spent opening windows is time not spent sheltering."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a mesocyclone?", ["A small tornado over water","A cyclone that forms in the mid-latitudes","The calm eye of a tornado","A rotating updraft inside a supercell thunderstorm"], 3, "Wind shear tilted upright by a strong updraft makes the updraft rotate, and strong tornadoes can form beneath it."],
    [1, "Why does the central United States get so many tornadoes?", ["It has the hottest summers on Earth","It lies on a tectonic plate boundary","Contrasting air masses meet over flat plains","Its forests channel the wind"], 2, "Humid Gulf air, dry western air and cold Canadian air collide over the plains, ideal for supercells."],
    [2, "How is a tornado's Enhanced Fujita rating usually decided?", ["By the damage it causes","By measuring its funnel width","By counting how long it lasts","By the colour of the funnel"], 0, "Winds are rarely measured directly, so ratings are estimated from the damage left behind."],
    [3, "What is the outback 'willy-willy' usually?", ["A small tornado from a thunderstorm","A tropical cyclone in miniature","A dust devil formed from hot ground, not a storm","A type of thunderstorm cloud"], 2, "Willy-willies are usually dust devils, which form on hot, clear days. True tornadoes need a thunderstorm above."],
    [4, "Why should you not open windows when a tornado approaches?", ["It lets the tornado come inside the house","It makes the tornado grow stronger","It increases the risk of lightning strikes","Pressure is not the danger; wind and debris are"], 3, "The pressure idea is a myth. Get to a small interior room on the lowest floor, away from windows."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The fastest winds ever measured near the ground are brief and tiny. Here's why."});

WB.L("antarctica", "geography", "beginner", "Antarctica: the continent of extremes",
  "The coldest, windiest, highest and driest continent holds most of the world's ice, and lakes hidden kilometres beneath it.",
  [
    ["A continent buried in ice", "Antarctica is almost twice the size of Australia and holds about 90 per cent of the world's ice.",
      "Antarctica covers about 14 million square kilometres, nearly twice the area of Australia. Almost all of it lies under an ice sheet averaging around two kilometres thick, and more than four kilometres deep in places. That ice holds roughly 90 per cent of all the ice on Earth and about 70 per cent of its fresh water. If it all melted, which would take many thousands of years, global sea levels would rise by close to 60 metres.\n\nThe ice is so heavy it presses the land beneath it downward, yet the ice sheet's thickness gives Antarctica the highest average elevation of any continent. Beneath it lie mountain ranges, valleys and even volcanoes."],
    ["Coldest and driest", "Antarctica holds the record for the lowest air temperature, and its interior is a desert with very little snowfall.",
      "On 21 July 1983, at Russia's Vostok Station on the high East Antarctic plateau, thermometers recorded -89.2 degrees Celsius, the coldest air temperature ever measured directly. Satellites have since detected snow surfaces below -90 degrees. Antarctica is colder than the Arctic because it is a high landmass rather than a frozen ocean, its white surface reflects most sunlight, and for months each winter the Sun does not rise over much of it.\n\nCold air holds little moisture, so the interior receives only a few centimetres of water equivalent a year. It counts as a desert. The ice built up because almost nothing melts. The McMurdo Dry Valleys, largely free of ice and snow, are used to test equipment and ideas for exploring Mars."],
    ["Home of the blizzard", "Cold, heavy air flowing downhill off the ice plateau creates some of the strongest winds on the planet.",
      "Antarctica's interior is a vast dome of ice, and the air above it becomes intensely cold and dense. Gravity pulls this heavy air downhill towards the coast, where it can accelerate into ferocious katabatic winds, from a Greek word meaning going down. Some coastal places are battered almost constantly.\n\nThe Australian geologist Douglas Mawson found this out during his Australasian Antarctic Expedition of 1911 to 1914. His base at Cape Denison in Commonwealth Bay was so wind-lashed that he titled his account The Home of the Blizzard. Expedition members learned to walk bent almost horizontally into the gusts. The same expedition saw Mawson's harrowing solo trek back to base after both his companions died."],
    ["A continent for science", "The Antarctic Treaty of 1959 set the continent aside for peace and science, and its claims are effectively frozen.",
      "Seven countries, including Australia, claim parts of Antarctica. Australia's claim, the Australian Antarctic Territory, is the largest at about 42 per cent of the continent. Yet these claims are effectively frozen.\n\nIn 1959, at the height of the Cold War, twelve nations signed the Antarctic Treaty, which came into force in 1961. It bans military activity and nuclear explosions, guarantees freedom of scientific research and requires countries to share their findings. The Madrid Protocol of 1991 later banned mining and set strict environmental rules. More than fifty countries have now joined. Australia runs three permanent research stations on the continent, Casey, Davis and Mawson, and a fourth on sub-Antarctic Macquarie Island."],
    ["Hidden lakes, hardy life", "Hundreds of liquid lakes lie beneath the ice, and the largest permanent land animal is a tiny wingless midge.",
      "Liquid water lies far beneath the ice. Radar surveys have found hundreds of subglacial lakes, kept from freezing by the insulating ice above and by heat rising from the Earth below. The largest known, Lake Vostok, lies under about four kilometres of ice, which Russian drillers reached in 2012. Some lakes are linked by rivers flowing under the ice sheet.\n\nOn the surface, life clings mostly to the coasts. Penguins, seals and seabirds feed in the rich surrounding ocean, and emperor penguins breed through the dark winter on the sea ice. There are no native land mammals. The largest animal living permanently on land is a wingless midge only a few millimetres long."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly how far would sea levels rise if all of Antarctica's ice melted?", ["About 6 metres","About 600 metres","About 60 metres","About 2 metres"], 2, "The ice sheet holds enough water to raise sea levels by close to 60 metres, though melting would take many thousands of years."],
    [1, "Why is Antarctica colder than the Arctic?", ["It is a high landmass, not a frozen ocean","It is farther from the Sun than the Arctic","It has more volcanoes cooling the air","It spins faster than the North Pole"], 0, "The Arctic is an ocean warmed slightly by the sea beneath, while Antarctica is a high, reflective continent."],
    [2, "What are katabatic winds?", ["Warm winds blowing in from the ocean","Winds caused by the Earth's rotation alone","Gusts produced by melting icebergs","Cold, dense air flowing downhill off the ice plateau"], 3, "Katabatic means going down. Heavy cold air drains off the high interior towards the coast, sometimes at great speed."],
    [3, "What does the Antarctic Treaty system do?", ["Divides Antarctica equally among all nations","Bans military activity and protects science","Gives Australia full ownership of the continent","Allows oil drilling with a permit"], 1, "The 1959 treaty bans military activity and guarantees scientific freedom, and the 1991 Madrid Protocol bans mining."],
    [4, "What is the largest animal living permanently on land in Antarctica?", ["The emperor penguin","The leopard seal","The Weddell seal","A tiny wingless midge"], 3, "Penguins and seals depend on the sea. The largest true land-dweller is a tiny flightless midge."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The coldest continent is also a desert. Why so little snow?"});

WB.L("everest-himalaya", "geography", "beginner", "Everest: the roof of the world",
  "The highest mountain on Earth is still rising, and its summit is made of rock that formed at the bottom of an ancient sea.",
  [
    ["How high is high?", "Everest's official height is 8,848.86 metres, agreed by Nepal and China in 2020, and the air there holds about a third of the oxygen at sea level.",
      "Mount Everest sits on the border between Nepal and the Tibet region of China. Measuring it has always been tricky, partly because its snow cap changes depth, and partly because the two countries used different methods and quoted slightly different figures for decades. In December 2020 they jointly announced a new official height of 8,848.86 metres, based on satellite positioning and surveyors carrying equipment to the summit.\n\nAt that height the air pressure is only about a third of that at sea level, so each breath delivers far less oxygen. Above about 8,000 metres lies what climbers call the death zone, where the body slowly deteriorates and cannot acclimatise."],
    ["Seashells on the summit", "Everest's summit rock formed on an ancient sea floor and was pushed skyward when India collided with Asia.",
      "The top of Everest is limestone that formed hundreds of millions of years ago on the floor of a shallow sea. Geologists have found fossils of marine creatures such as trilobites and crinoids (sea lilies) in it. How did a seabed end up nearly nine kilometres in the sky?\n\nThe answer is plate tectonics. India was once a separate landmass drifting north across the Tethys Ocean. When it collided with Asia, sediments from the ocean floor were crumpled, stacked and pushed upwards. The Himalaya are the result of that crash, which began around 50 million years ago, though geologists debate the exact timing. India still pushes north by a few centimetres every year."],
    ["A mountain still growing", "The Himalaya are pushed up a few millimetres a year while erosion wears them down, so their height is a constant tug of war.",
      "The collision has not stopped. Precise satellite measurements show parts of the Himalaya rising by a few millimetres each year. At the same time, ice, landslides, rivers and frost shatter the rock and carry it away, so the height we see is a constant tug of war between uplift and erosion.\n\nEarthquakes are part of the same process. The devastating Nepal earthquake of April 2015 was caused by the Indian plate sliding beneath Asia. It also shifted the land around Everest slightly, one reason the mountain was measured again a few years later."],
    ["Names and first ascent", "Everest was named after a surveyor who reportedly objected, and was first climbed on 29 May 1953 by Hillary and Tenzing.",
      "The mountain has local names: Chomolungma in Tibetan, often translated as goddess mother of the world, and Sagarmatha in Nepali. British surveyors called it Peak XV until calculations around 1852, in which Radhanath Sikdar is often credited with a key role, showed it to be the highest on Earth. Surveyor General Andrew Waugh proposed naming it after his predecessor, Sir George Everest, who reportedly objected that locals could not easily write or say it. The name was adopted in 1865 anyway.\n\nIn 1924 George Mallory and Andrew Irvine vanished near the top, and whether they summited is unknown. On 29 May 1953, Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay stood on the summit. Hundreds have died trying since."],
    ["Highest, but not tallest", "Mauna Kea is taller from base to peak and Chimborazo's summit is farthest from Earth's centre, so which is biggest depends on the question.",
      "Everest's summit is the highest point above sea level, but there are other ways to measure a mountain. Mauna Kea in Hawaii rises about 4,200 metres above the sea, yet most of the volcano is underwater. Measured from its base on the ocean floor, it stands around 10,000 metres, taller than Everest.\n\nThen there is Chimborazo, a volcano in Ecuador only about 6,260 metres above sea level. Because the Earth spins, it bulges slightly at the equator, and Chimborazo sits near the top of that bulge. Its summit is therefore the point on Earth's surface farthest from the planet's centre, roughly two kilometres farther out than Everest's. Which mountain is biggest depends on the question you ask."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does each breath deliver so little oxygen near Everest's summit?", ["The air contains no oxygen above 8,000 metres","Air pressure there is only about a third of that at sea level","Snow absorbs the oxygen","Climbers breathe more slowly in the cold"], 1, "Low air pressure means each breath carries far less oxygen, which is why the death zone is so punishing."],
    [1, "Why are there marine fossils near the summit of Everest?", ["Birds carried shells up there","Sea levels were once nine kilometres higher","The rock formed on a sea floor and was later uplifted","Climbers left them as offerings"], 2, "The summit limestone formed in the Tethys Ocean, and the India-Asia collision lifted it into the sky."],
    [2, "What is happening to the height of the Himalaya today?", ["They are sinking rapidly","They rise a few millimetres a year as erosion wears them","They stopped changing millions of years ago","They are rising by several metres a year"], 1, "India still pushes into Asia, lifting the range slowly, while erosion constantly removes rock."],
    [3, "What is still unknown about the 1924 attempt on Everest?", ["Which country sponsored it","Whether Mallory and Irvine reached the summit before dying","Whether the mountain had been surveyed","Which route they used"], 1, "Mallory and Irvine vanished high on the mountain, and the camera that might settle it has never been found."],
    [4, "Why is Chimborazo's summit farther from Earth's centre than Everest's?", ["It is taller above sea level","It is a volcano that is still growing","It sits near the equator, where the Earth bulges outwards","It stands on the ocean floor"], 2, "The spinning Earth bulges at the equator, so Chimborazo's summit is the farthest point from the planet's centre."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The summit of Everest is made of seabed. Here's how it got there."});

WB.L("lake-eyre", "geography", "beginner", "Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre: Australia's sometimes sea",
  "For most of its life it is a blinding sheet of salt. Then, every so often, distant floods turn it into Australia's largest lake and birds arrive from hundreds of kilometres away.",
  [
    ["Australia's lowest point", "Kati Thanda-Lake Eyre lies about 15 metres below sea level in outback South Australia and is usually a dry salt crust.",
      "In the arid north of South Australia lies Kati Thanda-Lake Eyre, the lowest natural point on the Australian continent, about 15 metres below sea level. Kati Thanda comes from the Arabana people, the traditional owners, for whom the lake holds deep cultural significance. The dual name was formally adopted in 2012. Lake Eyre honours the English-born explorer Edward John Eyre, who saw parts of it in 1840.\n\nMost of the time the lake is dry, a vast flat crust of salt that glares white under the sun. It has two main parts, Lake Eyre North, much the larger, and Lake Eyre South, joined by a narrow channel."],
    ["A continental drain", "The lake sits at the bottom of one of the world's largest internally draining basins, where no water ever reaches the sea.",
      "Water flows downhill, and in central Australia much of it flows towards Kati Thanda. Its catchment, the Lake Eyre Basin, covers about 1.2 million square kilometres, roughly one sixth of Australia, across parts of Queensland, the Northern Territory, South Australia and New South Wales.\n\nIt is one of the largest internally draining basins in the world, meaning none of its water ever reaches the sea. Rivers such as the Diamantina, the Georgina and Cooper Creek wander slowly across a vast, flat landscape known as the Channel Country. Floodwater can take months to travel from Queensland to the lake, and most of it evaporates or soaks into the ground on the way."],
    ["When the water comes", "The lake fills close to capacity only a few times a century, and even then it is shallow and turns saltier as it spreads.",
      "The basin is so dry and evaporation so intense that it takes exceptional rain to deliver serious water to Kati Thanda. Small floods reach the lake fairly often, and a significant filling happens only every several years. Filling close to capacity happens only a few times in a century. The great floods of the 1950s and 1974 were among the biggest on record.\n\nWhen full, it is the largest lake in Australia, covering around 9,500 square kilometres. Even then it is mostly just a few metres deep. As the water spreads over the salt crust, it dissolves salt and turns saltier, and the shallow water can glow pink from salt-loving algae and microbes."],
    ["An explosion of life", "Floods trigger a boom of shrimp, fish and waterbirds, but breeding is a race against the shrinking, saltier lake.",
      "When water arrives, the desert erupts with life. Eggs of tiny crustaceans such as brine shrimp, dormant in the dry mud, hatch within days. Fish washed down the rivers multiply in the fresher water. Then the birds come. Pelicans, silver gulls, terns, ducks and other waterbirds arrive in huge numbers, many to breed on islands in the lake.\n\nAustralian pelicans appear to fly hundreds of kilometres from the coast, and exactly how they detect that the lake has filled remains a mystery. Breeding is a race against time. As the lake shrinks and salt rises, fish die off and food runs out, and colonies may be abandoned before all the chicks fledge."],
    ["A record on the salt", "Lake Eyre's unregulated river system still floods in natural cycles, and in 1964 its dry bed hosted a land speed record.",
      "In 1964 British driver Donald Campbell brought his jet-turbine car, Bluebird CN7, to Lake Eyre. Unusual rain softened the salt and delayed his runs for weeks. On 17 July 1964, on a surface still damp, he set a land speed record of about 648 kilometres per hour (403 miles per hour). Later that year he set a water speed record in Western Australia, the only person to break both records in a year.\n\nThe basin itself is one of the largest unregulated dryland river systems left on Earth, flowing in irregular boom and bust cycles that drive fish breeding and green the Channel Country pastures. Scientists, pastoralists and Aboriginal communities who have cared for it for tens of thousands of years work to protect it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly how far below sea level is the lowest part of Kati Thanda-Lake Eyre?", ["About 150 metres","About 15 metres","About 1.5 metres","About 400 metres"], 1, "The lake's lowest point is about 15 metres below sea level, the lowest natural point in Australia."],
    [1, "What does it mean that the Lake Eyre Basin is 'internally draining'?", ["Its water flows underground to the ocean","It drains into the Murray River","Its water never reaches the sea","It is fed only by rain falling on the lake itself"], 2, "Water in the basin flows towards the lake and then evaporates or soaks away, never reaching the ocean."],
    [2, "How often does the lake fill close to its full capacity?", ["Only a few times a century","Every summer","Every two years","Never in recorded history"], 0, "Small floods reach the lake fairly often, but near-full filling happens only a few times each century."],
    [3, "What happens to the lake's birds and fish as the floodwater shrinks?", ["They multiply because the water is clearer","Salt rises, fish die off and colonies may be abandoned","They migrate back upstream","Nothing changes until the next flood"], 1, "As the lake shrinks and turns saltier, food runs out, so breeding is a race against time."],
    [4, "Why does the Lake Eyre Basin flood in irregular boom and bust cycles?", ["Its rivers are dammed and released on a schedule","Tides push seawater inland","Its rivers remain largely unregulated, following natural rainfall","Underground springs overflow each year"], 2, "Few dams or irrigation works interfere, so the rivers still flow as they always have, driving fish breeding and green pastures."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A salt crust the size of a small country sometimes becomes a lake. Why?"});

WB.L("supervolcanoes", "geography", "intermediate", "Supervolcanoes: the truth about Yellowstone",
  "A few volcanoes have erupted on a scale that dwarfs anything in recorded history. Are they overdue to do it again? The real answer is calmer than the headlines.",
  [
    ["What makes a supervolcano", "A supereruption ejects more than 1,000 cubic kilometres of material, leaving a vast collapsed caldera.",
      "Volcanologists measure eruptions on the Volcanic Explosivity Index, from 0 to 8. Each step up means roughly ten times more material. A VEI 8 eruption blasts out more than 1,000 cubic kilometres of ash, pumice and rock. For comparison, Mount St Helens in 1980 produced around one cubic kilometre.\n\nA volcano capable of this is called a supervolcano, a popular rather than strictly scientific term that spread through television documentaries around the year 2000. Supervolcanoes rarely look like classic cones. Their eruptions empty such a vast underground magma reservoir that the ground collapses, leaving an enormous depression called a caldera."],
    ["Yellowstone's past", "Yellowstone has had three giant caldera-forming eruptions in 2.1 million years, and it sits over a moving hotspot.",
      "Yellowstone, mostly in Wyoming, has produced three huge caldera-forming eruptions in 2.1 million years. The first, about 2.1 million years ago, was the largest, at around 2,500 cubic kilometres. The second, about 1.3 million years ago, was much smaller. The third, about 631,000 years ago, produced around 1,000 cubic kilometres and formed today's caldera. Since then it has mostly produced slow lava flows, the latest about 70,000 years ago.\n\nWhy is it volcanic, far from any plate boundary? A hotspot, a plume of hot rock rising from the mantle, stays put while the North American plate slides south west over it. Older calderas trail across Idaho's Snake River Plain. Yellowstone holds more than half of the world's active geysers, including Old Faithful."],
    ["Is Yellowstone overdue?", "No: volcanoes do not erupt on a timetable, and the chance of a supereruption in any given year is very small.",
      "A common claim online is that Yellowstone is overdue, because its big eruptions average about 700,000 years apart. This reasoning fails. Three events are far too few to establish a pattern, the gaps were quite different, and volcanoes are not clocks.\n\nStudies of the magma beneath Yellowstone suggest the reservoir is mostly solid crystal mush, with only a modest fraction of molten rock, far less than a supereruption would likely need. The United States Geological Survey monitors the area closely with seismometers, GPS and gas sensors. It considers a future supereruption possible but highly unlikely in any given year, and judges smaller lava flows or steam explosions much more likely."],
    ["Toba and the human story", "The Toba supereruption, about 74,000 years ago, was once blamed for nearly wiping out humanity, but newer evidence weakens that idea.",
      "The largest eruption of the past couple of million years happened at Toba, on Sumatra, about 74,000 years ago. It ejected something like 2,800 cubic kilometres and left a caldera now filled by Lake Toba, about 100 kilometres long.\n\nIn the 1990s some researchers proposed that Toba caused a volcanic winter so severe it pushed early humans to the brink of extinction, a genetic bottleneck. The idea became famous, but newer evidence has weakened it. Archaeological sites in India and South Africa suggest people carried on making tools and finding food through the eruption. Many scientists now think Toba's climate effects were real but shorter or more patchy than once feared."],
    ["Closer to home: Taupō", "New Zealand's Lake Taupō fills the caldera of the youngest known supereruption, about 25,500 years ago.",
      "The world's most recent supereruption happened not far from Australia. About 25,500 years ago, the Taupō volcano in New Zealand's North Island produced the Oruanui eruption, ejecting more than 1,000 cubic kilometres of material. Ash fell over much of New Zealand and as far as the Chatham Islands. Lake Taupō, New Zealand's largest lake, now fills part of the collapsed caldera.\n\nTaupō erupted again around 232 CE in the Hatepe eruption, one of the most violent of the past few thousand years, though not on a super scale. It remains an active system that scientists watch carefully. Like Yellowstone, its quiet beauty, with hot springs and steaming vents, sits on top of an extraordinary history."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What defines a supereruption on the Volcanic Explosivity Index?", ["Any eruption lasting more than a year","An eruption that produces lava flows over 100 kilometres long","An eruption ejecting more than 1,000 cubic kilometres of material","Any eruption that kills more than 10,000 people"], 2, "A VEI 8 eruption ejects more than 1,000 cubic kilometres, about a thousand times more than Mount St Helens in 1980."],
    [1, "Why is there a line of old calderas across southern Idaho?", ["Yellowstone was once several volcanoes on a plate boundary","The North American plate has moved over a stationary hotspot","Glaciers carried volcanic rock there","Rivers carved out the calderas"], 1, "As the plate drifted south west over the mantle hotspot, activity appeared to move north east, leaving older calderas behind."],
    [2, "Why is the claim that Yellowstone is 'overdue' misleading?", ["Yellowstone erupted in 1980","Its eruptions happen exactly every 100,000 years","Yellowstone is extinct","Volcanoes do not erupt on a schedule"], 3, "Eruption gaps vary, volcanoes are not clocks, and the magma reservoir appears mostly solid crystal mush."],
    [3, "What has newer evidence suggested about Toba's effect on humans?", ["It wiped out most humans outside Africa","Archaeology suggests people carried on through it","It caused humans to migrate to Australia","It had no effect on climate at all"], 1, "Archaeological sites suggest people coped, weakening the idea of a near-extinction bottleneck."],
    [4, "Where was the world's most recent known supereruption?", ["Yellowstone, USA","Toba, Indonesia","Mount Vesuvius, Italy","Taupō, New Zealand"], 3, "The Oruanui eruption at Taupō, about 25,500 years ago, is the youngest known supereruption."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Is Yellowstone overdue to blow? The answer is calmer than the headlines."});

WB.L("green-sahara", "geography", "intermediate", "When the Sahara was green",
  "Ten thousand years ago, people fished, swam and herded cattle across land that is now the world's largest hot desert. A wobble in Earth's orbit explains how.",
  [
    ["Hippos in the desert", "Rock art, fossils and ancient shorelines show the Sahara once had lakes, grasslands and large animals.",
      "The Sahara covers around nine million square kilometres of North Africa, larger than Australia, and much of it gets almost no rain. Yet clues to a different past are scattered across it. Rock paintings and engravings at places like Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria show giraffes, elephants, rhinoceroses, hippopotamuses and herds of cattle. Fossilised crocodile and fish bones lie in bare sand, and ancient shorelines mark vanished lakes. Mega-Lake Chad once covered hundreds of thousands of square kilometres.\n\nScientists call this wetter time the African Humid Period, lasting roughly from 11,000 to 5,000 years ago. In 1933 explorer László Almásy found the Cave of Swimmers in Egypt's Gilf Kebir, whose figures appear to swim, though some researchers think they may be spiritual rather than literal."],
    ["The orbital wobble", "Slow changes in Earth's orbit strengthened summer sunshine and pulled the monsoon rains north.",
      "The main driver was astronomical. Earth's axis slowly wobbles, like a spinning top, in a cycle of roughly 20,000 to 26,000 years called precession. This changes the time of year when Earth is closest to the Sun. Around 10,000 years ago the Northern Hemisphere was closest during its summer, so North Africa received noticeably more summer sunshine than today.\n\nStronger heating over the land drew the West African monsoon much further north, carrying rain deep into what is now desert. Feedbacks amplified the change: plants and lakes darkened the landscape and recycled moisture into the air, helping the rains persist. The opposite process eventually dried the region out."],
    ["People of the green Sahara", "Fishers and herders lived across the wet Sahara, leaving graves, pottery and harpoons behind.",
      "People moved into the Sahara as it greened. At Gobero, in Niger, archaeologists working from 2000 onwards uncovered graves of communities who lived beside an ancient lake. Their remains lay with bone harpoons, fish hooks and pottery, along with bones of fish, turtles and crocodiles. One moving burial held a woman and two children, apparently laid to rest together.\n\nAcross the region people made some of the world's early pottery and, later, took up cattle herding as conditions became drier and less predictable. Rock art shows people with herds and dogs. This was not a single culture but many groups, adapting over thousands of years to a landscape that kept changing around them."],
    ["Drying out", "The Sahara became desert again around 5,000 years ago, though how fast is still debated.",
      "As Earth's orbit continued its slow cycle, summer sunshine in North Africa weakened and the monsoon retreated south. The lakes shrank, the grasslands thinned and the Sahara became desert again around 5,000 years ago.\n\nScientists still debate how quickly. Ocean sediments, which record dust blown off Africa, suggest a fairly abrupt shift over a few centuries. Lake and pollen records suggest a more gradual drying that varied by place. The people there had to adapt, and many appear to have moved towards more reliable water, including the Nile Valley. Some archaeologists suggest this migration played a part in the rise of early Egyptian civilisation, though that link remains a hypothesis."],
    ["A desert that breathes", "The Sahara has swung between green and desert many times, and its dust still fertilises the Amazon.",
      "The green period was not unique. Sediment records show the Sahara has swung between desert and grassland many times over hundreds of thousands of years, roughly in step with the precession cycle. These green corridors may have opened routes for animals and early humans to move out of Africa.\n\nEven as a desert, the Sahara shapes the planet. Winds lift hundreds of millions of tonnes of dust each year and carry it across the Atlantic. Much comes from the Bodele Depression in Chad, the dried bed of the old mega-lake. Satellite studies suggest this dust delivers phosphorus and other nutrients to the Amazon rainforest, helping fertilise one of Earth's richest ecosystems."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which of these is evidence that the Sahara was once green?", ["Ancient tree rings in the sand","Records written by Roman travellers","Coal deposits under the dunes","Rock art showing hippos, giraffes and cattle"], 3, "Rock art at sites like Tassili n'Ajjer, plus fossils of crocodiles and fish, show the Sahara once had water and grasslands."],
    [1, "What mainly caused the green Sahara?", ["A change in ocean salt levels","A wobble in Earth's orbit strengthening the monsoon","A series of volcanic eruptions","Humans planting forests"], 1, "Precession gave North Africa stronger summer sunshine, drawing the West African monsoon far further north."],
    [2, "What did archaeologists find at Gobero in Niger?", ["A buried city with pyramids and temples","The ruins of a Roman fort and its road","Lakeside graves with harpoons, hooks and pottery","Fossilised nests of dinosaur eggs"], 2, "Gobero's burials sat beside an ancient lake and included fishing tools and bones of fish, turtles and crocodiles."],
    [3, "What do scientists still debate about the end of the green Sahara?", ["Whether it ever happened","Whether the Moon caused it","Whether it ended within the last century","How quickly the drying occurred"], 3, "Some records suggest an abrupt shift over centuries, others a gradual drying that varied by place."],
    [4, "How does Saharan dust affect South America today?", ["It causes frequent dust storms in Brazil","It blocks rainfall over the Amazon","It helps fertilise the Amazon rainforest with nutrients such as phosphorus","It has no effect because it never crosses the Atlantic"], 2, "Dust, much of it from Chad's Bodele Depression, crosses the Atlantic and delivers nutrients to the Amazon."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"People once swam and herded cattle where the Sahara now lies. What changed?"});

WB.L("monsoons", "geography", "intermediate", "Monsoons: the seasons of rain",
  "For billions of people, the most important weather event of the year is the arrival of the monsoon. Its rains decide harvests, rivers and even history.",
  [
    ["More than rain", "A monsoon is a seasonal reversal of winds that brings a wet season and a dry season, and the word means season.",
      "The word monsoon comes, through Portuguese and Dutch, from the Arabic mawsim, meaning season. People often use it to mean heavy rain, but strictly a monsoon is a pattern of winds that reverses direction with the seasons. For part of the year, winds blow in from the ocean, carrying moisture and bringing a long wet season. For the rest, they blow from the land, bringing dry weather.\n\nMonsoons affect South Asia, East and South East Asia, West Africa, northern Australia and parts of the Americas. The South Asian monsoon is the largest and most famous, and it shapes the lives and livelihoods of well over a billion people."],
    ["How the engine works", "Land heats faster than sea, so in summer hot land draws moist ocean air inland where it rises and falls as rain.",
      "The basic driver is simple. Land heats and cools much faster than the ocean. In late spring and summer the land warms strongly, and the hot air above it rises, creating lower pressure near the ground. Cooler, moist air flows in from the ocean to replace it. As that humid air is lifted, by heating or by mountains, it cools and drops its moisture as rain. In winter the pattern reverses, as the land becomes cooler than the sea.\n\nThe real picture is more complicated. The monsoon is also tied to the seasonal migration of the tropical rain belt, the Intertropical Convergence Zone, which follows the Sun's strongest heating north and south through the year."],
    ["India's great monsoon", "The South West monsoon reaches Kerala around 1 June and brings roughly three quarters of India's yearly rain.",
      "Each year the India Meteorological Department watches for the monsoon to arrive on the coast of Kerala, at India's southern tip, typically around 1 June. Over the following weeks the rains push north and usually cover almost the whole country by early to mid-July. The South West monsoon delivers roughly three quarters of India's annual rainfall in just four months.\n\nThe Himalaya and the high Tibetan Plateau play a crucial role. The plateau heats strongly in summer, helping power the circulation, while the mountains keep the moisture over the subcontinent. A good monsoon fills reservoirs, recharges groundwater and feeds rice and other crops. A weak one can mean drought, falling harvests and rising food prices."],
    ["The wettest places on Earth", "Monsoon winds forced up steep hills in north east India produce some of the highest rainfall ever recorded.",
      "Some of the wettest places on Earth are in the Khasi Hills of Meghalaya, in north east India. Monsoon winds loaded with moisture from the Bay of Bengal are forced up steep slopes and wring out torrential rain. The village of Mawsynram averages close to 12 metres of rain a year, among the highest averages anywhere. Nearby Cherrapunji holds a famous record: in the twelve months from August 1860 to July 1861 it received more than 26 metres.\n\nPeople have adapted in remarkable ways. Some Khasi villages have grown living bridges by training the roots of rubber fig trees across rivers over many years, creating structures that grow stronger with age."],
    ["Trade winds and Australia", "Reliable monsoon winds shaped Indian Ocean trade for centuries, and northern Australia has its own monsoon.",
      "Because monsoon winds blow reliably one way for part of the year and the other way later, a ship could sail across the Arabian Sea and return when the winds reversed. Arab, Indian, Persian, African, and later Greek and Roman merchants used this to trade spices, pepper, textiles, gems and gold between East Africa, Arabia, India and South East Asia. A first century Greek guidebook, the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, describes these routes.\n\nAustralia has a monsoon too. Across the Top End, the Kimberley and Cape York, the wet season runs roughly November or December to March, with north westerly winds, heavy rain and humidity, arriving in active bursts and breaks. First Nations peoples of the north recognise many more seasons than two, based on detailed knowledge of weather, plants and animals."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Strictly speaking, what is a monsoon?", ["Any spell of very heavy rain","Winds that reverse with the seasons","A powerful tropical cyclone","A steady wind off the sea"], 1, "The word comes from the Arabic for season, because the winds reverse with the seasons."],
    [1, "What is the basic driver of a summer monsoon?", ["Strong tides pulled by the Moon","Volcanic heat beneath the land","Cold air sinking over the ocean","Land heating faster than the sea"], 3, "Hot land creates low pressure, and moist ocean air flows in, rises and drops its rain."],
    [2, "Roughly what share of India's annual rainfall comes from the South West monsoon?", ["About a tenth","About a quarter","About half","About three quarters"], 3, "The South West monsoon delivers roughly three quarters of India's annual rain in about four months."],
    [3, "Why are places like Mawsynram and Cherrapunji so wet?", ["They sit at sea level beside the ocean","Moist winds are forced up steep hills","They lie directly on the equator","Glacier meltwater floods them each year"], 1, "Moisture from the Bay of Bengal is pushed up the Khasi Hills, wringing out enormous rainfall."],
    [4, "How did monsoon winds help ancient trade?", ["They pushed fleets across the Pacific Ocean","They kept the Indian Ocean calm all year","Seasonal reversals let ships sail out and home","They blew ships straight to Europe in weeks"], 2, "Sailors timed voyages so the winds carried them one way, then waited for the reversal to sail home."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For a billion people, one wind decides the year. How does it turn around?"});

WB.L("ice-cores", "geography", "advanced", "Ice cores: reading Earth's frozen diary",
  "Drill deep into Antarctica and you pull up ancient air, volcanic fallout and the planet's temperature history, layer by layer, going back 800,000 years.",
  [
    ["Snow that becomes a record", "Snow that never melts compresses into ice, forming a dated archive with the oldest layers at the bottom.",
      "In the coldest parts of Greenland and Antarctica, snow falls and never melts. Each year's snow buries the last, compressing into granular firn and then solid ice. The youngest ice sits on top and the oldest near the bedrock, sometimes more than three kilometres down. Scientists drill out cores about ten centimetres wide and ship them frozen to laboratories.\n\nWhere snowfall is heavy, each year leaves a distinct layer, so layers can be counted like tree rings for tens of thousands of years. Deeper down, the ice thins and flows, so ages come from flow models and volcanic markers. Eruptions such as Tambora in 1815 leave sulphate spikes in both Greenland and Antarctic ice, tying the records to one calendar."],
    ["Bubbles of ancient air", "Bubbles sealed in ice are real samples of the past atmosphere, though the air is younger than the ice around it.",
      "The most remarkable treasure in ice cores is air. As firn turns to ice, the spaces between grains close off and trap tiny bubbles of atmosphere. In the laboratory, scientists crush or melt samples under vacuum and measure gases such as carbon dioxide and methane.\n\nThere is one subtlety. Air keeps circulating through the porous firn until it is sealed, typically several tens of metres down. So the trapped air is younger than the ice around it, sometimes by thousands of years where snowfall is slow. Scientists correct for the gap. Australian researchers contributed greatly with cores from Law Dome, near Casey Station, where high snowfall gives a detailed record of carbon dioxide over the past two thousand years."],
    ["A thermometer in the water", "The ratio of heavy to light water molecules in each layer of ice reveals how cold it was when the snow fell.",
      "Ice itself records temperature. Some water molecules contain heavier isotopes, such as oxygen-18 instead of oxygen-16, or deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen. As moist air travels towards the poles and cools, the heavier molecules tend to fall out as rain or snow first.\n\nSo the colder the conditions, the fewer heavy molecules remain in the snow that finally lands on the ice sheet. By measuring the ratio of heavy to light isotopes in each layer, scientists can reconstruct how temperature changed over time. This isotope thermometer, developed largely by Danish scientist Willi Dansgaard from the 1950s, is a foundation of modern climate science."],
    ["800,000 years of climate", "Antarctic cores show eight ice-age cycles in which carbon dioxide and temperature rose and fell together.",
      "The longest continuous ice core record comes from Dome C, high on the East Antarctic plateau, drilled by the European project EPICA. It reaches back about 800,000 years and shows eight full cycles of ice ages and warmer interglacial periods.\n\nThrough all of them, carbon dioxide and temperature rise and fall together. In cold glacial periods carbon dioxide sat around 180 parts per million. In warm interglacials it rose to around 280 to 300. Changes in Earth's orbit pace the cycles, with carbon dioxide acting as a powerful amplifier. By the mid-2020s atmospheric carbon dioxide had passed 420 parts per million, far outside the range of the entire ice core record."],
    ["Abrupt change, older ice", "Greenland ice shows climate can shift by many degrees within decades, and scientists are now hunting for ice over a million years old.",
      "Greenland cores revealed something unsettling. In the last ice age, temperatures there repeatedly jumped by several degrees, sometimes ten or more, within decades. These Dansgaard–Oeschger events showed that climate can change abruptly. The ice also records human history: lead from Roman mining and smelting settled in the snow, and dipped in times of war and plague. Wildfires and dust storms leave traces too.\n\nA puzzle remains. About a million years ago, ice-age cycles lengthened from roughly 41,000 to about 100,000 years, and nobody is sure why. In early 2025 the Beyond EPICA project reached bedrock at Little Dome C, recovering ice believed to be well over a million years old."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do scientists date ice too deep for its yearly layers to be counted?", ["By drilling a second hole beside the first","By measuring the ice's temperature at depth","By using ice-flow models and volcanic markers","By counting the bubbles in each metre of core"], 2, "Deep ice is squeezed thin, so ages come from flow models tied to markers like volcanic sulphate spikes."],
    [1, "Why is the air in ice core bubbles younger than the ice around it?", ["Bubbles slowly rise through the solid ice","Air circulates through porous firn until it is sealed off","Modern air leaks into cores during drilling","Bacteria make new gases inside the ice"], 1, "Air stays mobile in the firn until it seals off, so the trapped sample is younger than the ice, and scientists correct for the gap."],
    [2, "How do isotope ratios in ice reveal past temperatures?", ["Warmer snow always contains more salt","Colder conditions leave fewer heavy molecules in snow","Heavy isotopes glow under ultraviolet light","Colder ice forms thicker layers every year"], 1, "Heavier molecules fall out first as air cools on its way to the poles, so colder snow holds fewer of them."],
    [3, "What does the 800,000-year record show about carbon dioxide and temperature?", ["They moved independently throughout the record","They rose and fell together across eight cycles","Carbon dioxide stayed constant for 800,000 years","Carbon dioxide rose only when ice sheets grew"], 1, "Across eight cycles carbon dioxide tracked temperature between about 180 and 300 parts per million, well below today's level."],
    [4, "What did Dansgaard–Oeschger events show?", ["That ice ages always end very gradually","That Greenland was never covered in thick ice","That volcanoes are the main cause of ice ages","That climate can shift abruptly within decades"], 3, "Greenland ice showed that climate can change suddenly, not only slowly."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Antarctic ice holds air from 800,000 years ago, and it's still telling us things."});

